 ## Overview
-This method computes the ensemble-averaged transmittance of non-exponential participating media by modeling the line-averaged micro-scale density as a random variable and evaluating its characteristic function (CF) [S1]. It solves the problem of calculating light transport in media with spatial correlations (non-exponential decay) without the computational cost of instantiating discrete particles [S1]. Unlike the [Non-exponential transport kernel via macroscopic optical depth](14530-non-exponential-transport-kernel-via-macroscopic-optical-dep.md) which relies on macroscopic optical depth approximations, this approach derives closed-form transmittance directly from the probability distribution of the density fluctuations [S1].
+This method computes the ensemble-averaged transmittance of non-exponential media by modeling the line-averaged micro-scale density as a random variable and evaluating its characteristic function (CF) [S1]. It solves the problem of computing light transport in heterogeneous media with spatial correlations without instantiating billions of discrete particles, providing a closed-form alternative to tabulation-based approaches [S1]. Unlike the [Non-exponential transport kernel via macroscopic optical depth](14530-non-exponential-transport-kernel-via-macroscopic-optical-dep.md) which relies on macroscopic optical depth approximations, this method explicitly derives the transmittance from the probability distribution of the line-averaged extinction coefficient [S1].
 
